In `MoeFCGemm::run_kernel_` (`csrc/nv_internal/tensorrt_llm/cutlass_extensions/include/cutlass_extensions/gemm/kernel/moe_cutlass_kernel.h`, v0.7.0 and current main):

```cpp
typename Mma::IteratorA iterator_A(
    LayoutA(ldm_A), ptr_A, {problem_size.m(), problem_size.k()}, thread_idx, tb_offset_A);
```

`LayoutA(ldm_A)` turns into a temporary `IteratorA::Params` that is destroyed at the end of the statement, but `PredicatedTileAccessIterator` keeps `Params const &params_`, so every later `++iterator_A` / `add_tile_offset` reads a dead object. Same for `iterator_B`, and for the scale iterator built with `initialize_scale(LayoutScaleZero(ldm_Scale), ...)` in the dq path.

With nvcc the stack bytes happen to survive and it works. With clang at -O2/-O3 the loads from the dead object are treated as undefined and most of the cp.async copies that load A and B read from garbage addresses. We get `CUDA_ERROR_ILLEGAL_ADDRESS` on the first bf16 `cutlass_fused_moe` call (DeepSeek-V2-Lite shapes, sm_121a), and compute-sanitizer reports invalid 16-byte global reads in `MoeFCGemm<MmaMultistage<GemmShape<32,128,64>, ...>>`. In clang's PTX for `moe_gemm_kernels_bf16_bf16.cu`, 2370 of the 2520 `cp.async` copies (in all 75 kernels that use `cp.async`) take their source address from a register that is never written, at -O2 and at -O3; at -O1, none do. With the change below, none do at -O3.

Giving the Params a name removes the illegal address for us (compute-sanitizer memcheck on the same call: 1356 errors before, every one it printed an invalid 16-byte global read; no memory errors after):

```cpp
typename Mma::IteratorA::Params params_A{LayoutA(ldm_A)};  // braces: with parens this declares a function
typename Mma::IteratorA iterator_A(
    params_A, ptr_A, {problem_size.m(), problem_size.k()}, thread_idx, tb_offset_A);
typename Mma::IteratorB::Params params_B{LayoutB(ldm_B)};
typename Mma::IteratorB iterator_B(
    params_B, ptr_B, {problem_size.k() * kInterleave, problem_size.n() / kInterleave}, thread_idx, tb_offset_B);
// dq path
typename Mma::IteratorScale::Params params_scale{LayoutScaleZero(ldm_Scale)};
```

With this change the call no longer faults, but correct output under clang also needed a second fix, in `moe_cute_util.cuh` (`util_convert_type` returns a view of a local array; FlashInfer carries the same code in its copy of that file), reported in https://github.com/NVIDIA/TensorRT-LLM/issues/19943.

TensorRT-LLM has the same file and the same code: https://github.com/NVIDIA/TensorRT-LLM/issues/19944. CUTLASS's own `gemm/kernel/gemm_grouped.h` uses the same pattern.

`launchFusedAdaptiveLayerNormKernel` (`cpp/tensorrt_llm/kernels/fusedAdaptiveLayerNormKernel.cu`) computes the variance in FP32 as `E[x²] - mean²`. For a row whose mean is large relative to its spread, cancellation can make `variance + eps` negative, and the whole output row becomes NaN for finite BF16 input.

**Example**

Width 5120, BF16: two values 15.6875 followed by 5,118 values 15.75. The exact variance is about 1.53e-6, so the output should be finite. Built from source (`a1f562a`; main `e2852d9` has the same kernel) and called through its launcher on an A100, the kernel returns NaN in all 5,120 columns, in plain, affine and modulation modes, for eps 1e-6 and 1e-5. Control rows (constant 1, and balanced ±1) are correct.

Through the op, in builds that include it (TensorRT-LLM 1.2.1 does not yet, so we ran the launcher directly):

```python
import torch
import tensorrt_llm  # registers torch.ops.trtllm

x = torch.full((1, 5120), 15.75, dtype=torch.bfloat16, device="cuda")
x[:, :2] = 15.6875
y = torch.ops.trtllm.fused_adaptive_layernorm(x, None, None, None, None, 0, 1e-6)
x64 = x.cpu().double()
mean = x64.mean(-1, keepdim=True)
variance = ((x64 - mean) ** 2).mean(-1, keepdim=True)
eps = torch.tensor(1e-6, dtype=torch.float32).double()
reference = (x64 - mean) / torch.sqrt(variance + eps)
print("output_nan_count", torch.isnan(y).sum().item())
print("reference_is_finite", torch.isfinite(reference).all().item())
```

Expected `output_nan_count 0`.

**Reach**

The public op accepts any contiguous BF16 `[M, 5120]` input, and Wan's dense normalization calls this kernel at width 5120. We have not measured how often real activations reach this regime.

**Proposed fix**

Compute the mean first, then the variance from the cached values centered on it:

```diff
